>
<p>An enterprise SEO audit needs a custom scope because page volume, templates, rendering, regional sites and ownership change the evidence required. Page count alone does not define an enterprise engagement.</p>
<p>AuditWeb scopes enterprise work around business-critical segments, available logs and Search Console data, release risks and stakeholder decisions. The quote should state samples, exclusions and deliverables.</p>

<h2 id="what-changes-at-enterprise-scale">What changes in an enterprise SEO audit?</h2>
<p>Enterprise audits analyse segments and systems before individual URLs. The plan should separate product, category, editorial, market and utility templates so a large crawl does not hide a high-impact pattern.</p>
<p>Google states that crawl demand and host capacity influence crawling in its <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/large-site-managing-crawl-budget">large-site crawl-budget guidance</a>. Log analysis can show requested URLs. It does not prove that every unrequested URL has a crawl-budget problem.</p>

<h2 id="whats-included">Which inputs and samples should the scope define?</h2>
<p>The scope should define crawl limits, template samples, markets, user agents, date ranges and access before collection begins. Optional inputs include server logs, Search Console exports, analytics and release records.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Crawl and indexation:</strong> response codes, robots rules, canonicals, sitemaps and internal discovery by segment.</li>
  <li><strong>Rendering:</strong> source and rendered HTML for representative JavaScript templates.</li>
  <li><strong>International:</strong> agreed locale clusters, alternate annotations and duplicate-content questions.</li>
  <li><strong>Performance:</strong> field and lab evidence for named templates with the measurement source recorded.</li>
</ul>
<p>Google explains that it crawls, renders and indexes JavaScript pages in separate phases in its <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/javascript/javascript-seo-basics">JavaScript SEO guide</a>. Test representative rendered output rather than assuming all templates behave alike.</p>

<h2 id="stakeholder-reporting">What should enterprise stakeholders receive?</h2>
<p>Each audience should receive the detail needed for its decision. Engineering needs reproducible evidence and acceptance criteria.</p>
<p>Marketing needs affected segments and dependencies. Leadership needs confirmed risks, choices and limits.</p>
<p>Agree whether the engagement includes a findings database, executive summary, implementation workshop or presentation. AuditWeb does not treat every format as an automatic deliverable.</p>

<h2 id="pricing-and-timeline">How are enterprise price and timing set?</h2>
<p>Enterprise audits use a custom quote. Price and timing depend on URL volume, template variety, data preparation, markets, rendering tests and stakeholder outputs.</p>
<p>Provide a rough indexable-page count, platform, countries, known incident and required decision. AuditWeb will then confirm the sample, access, price, delivery date and review meetings in writing.</p>

<h2 id="faq">What should an enterprise team confirm?</h2>
<h3>Is log-file analysis always included?</h3><p>No. Add log-file analysis when the audit question needs request-level crawler evidence and suitable logs are available.</p>
<h3>Does AuditWeb need production credentials?</h3><p>Access depends on scope. Prefer exports or time-limited read-only access. Never send passwords through the public form.</p>
<h3>Can one crawl cover every enterprise SEO question?</h3><p>No. A crawl records retrievable URLs and technical signals. Indexation, traffic, revenue and release questions require separate evidence.</p>
